Before any wire, base-conversion residual, or challenged view opens, coin-flipped inner-product sacrifices verify all multiplications: in `outer_mpc.rs`, two-challenge `GF(2^128)` Boolean AND checks (`BIT_CHECK_NUMERATOR = 63` across up to `4,096` batches) bound soundness error below $(63 slash (2^128 - 1))^2 < 2^(-244)$ per flush, paired with $<= 35 slash (p - 1) < 2^(-249)$ large-prime checks and $<= 35 slash (p^3 - 1) < 2^(-186)$ cubic-Goldilocks checks, followed by a shared output zero test (`ActiveMigrationZeroTest.lean`, which proves `tooth_premature_residual_open_recovers_spending_key` and currently verifies retired numerator `54` pending constant sync; multi-round QROM extraction for 438-rep ZKBoo remains open).

*Threshold ML-DSA-44/65/87 and QROM `BlindVC` credentials (`qsag-cred`).* Dealerless threshold lattice signing supports all three NIST FIPS 204 security levels (@mldsa) for committees of $n <= 64$ custodians with $t >= 2$ and $n >= 2t-1$: key material is $(t,n)$-Shamir shared, and each signature is produced by a quorum of $2t - 1 <= 16$ custodians (hence $t <= 8$) with an honest majority inside the quorum, who convert their Shamir shares into the replicated sharing of the signing engine under multiplication and share-conversion checks (`threshold_mldsa_shamir.rs`). Arithmetic is accelerated by Seiler's signed-Montgomery SIMD NTT [Sei18] ($q = 8,380,417$). Custodians rejection-sample short secret shares in $[-eta, eta]$ inside RSS and evaluate online norm/hint bounds (`check_mldsa44_norm_bounds`, `check_mldsa65_norm_bounds`) across $2t - 1$ active signers. `threshold_mldsa.rs` and `ThresholdMlDsaSecurity.lean` enforce *conditional sealed-$bold(w)_1$ abort-transcript view privacy* (`threshold_mldsa_abort_transcript_zk`): high bits $bold(w)_1$ and challenge $c$ stay secret-shared inside RSS until norm and hint checks pass, while `MlDsaReleaseGate` OTP-masks partial responses $bold(z)_i mod q$ under `cSHAKE256` and wipes buffers on rejection—proving zero statistical distance (`SD = 0`, instantiated at ML-DSA-44 dimensions) conditioned on public accept/reject outcomes while durably burning nonces (full retry-count simulation, rectangular `65/87` matrices, and $[-gamma_1 + 1, gamma_1]$ boundary alignment remain open). For privacy-preserving credentials, `PiVoprf` implements a 2-of-5 Poseidon2 TOPRF with constructive unique line decoding against one Byzantine server (`toprf_2of5_byzantine_unique_decoding`, extracted `toprf_majority_of_3_goldilocks`), alongside a Shamir–SPDZ backend for $2 <= t <= n <= 64$ (`voprf_spdz.rs`), backing `PiBlindVc` six-limb attribute commitments. The SPDZ backend evaluates from a reservoir of preprocessed power tuples, each reached through a cursor that advances before the range is returned, so no tuple is used twice; the input-binding opening, one batched MAC check over every S-box opening, and the output opening are each MAC-checked in that order, and outputs are further padded by client masks no server knows. A refusal is classified: `Refusal::Request` (wrong context, a non-canonical word, or two maskings of one limb that disagree, all detected before any circuit tuple is used) spends only that session's reservation, whereas `Refusal::Quorum` poisons the reservoir and wipes the session's MAC-key shares—so a malformed client request can no longer destroy the servers' preprocessing. Preprocessing runs in chunks of 4 power tuples or 32 triples under separate session identifiers, each keeping the full statistical parameter with a union bound over chunks: four evaluations for a 2-of-3 quorum preprocess in `6.2 s` where a single run took `201 s` (`32×`), and one online evaluation takes `0.23–0.28 ms` (`0.33–1.0 ms` at 3-of-5). An $(8, 64)$ deployment was run end to end: 64-server enrollment in `295 s`, two evaluations preprocessed among 8 servers in `64.5 s`, and `0.92–0.99 ms` per online evaluation, all servers in one process. Issuance and presentation use different proof systems. *Blind issuance* proves the blinded commitment with 438 repetitions of three-party Goldilocks ZKBoo ($(2 slash 3)^438 < 2^(-256)$); for the relation restricted to credential validity, `BlindVcQromSecurity.lean` and `BlindVcCollisionFinder.lean` prove `438`-repetition QROM Fiat–Shamir soundness ($< 2^(-128)$ for $Q <= 2^60$, `blindVc_quantum128_qrom_fs_soundness`) and constructive extraction of either a valid witness or a `Type`-valued finite-table commitment conflict (`verifyOpenedBlindVcRep_extract3_finite_conflict`). The Rust issuer authenticates the canonical issuance message with a TOPRF evaluation, whereas the Lean reduction `blindVc_presentation_reduces_to_issuer_mldsa` is stated for an ML-DSA-authenticated issuer. *Selective-disclosure presentations* prove that the holder's commitment lies under the depth-20 Poseidon2 accumulator root, that the public presentation commitment rerandomizes it, that the scope tag is well formed, and that the disclosed attributes match. Their MPC-in-the-head form runs 33 repetitions of a 256-party seed-tree protocol with a polynomial-multiplication check over $"GF"(p^5) = FF_p [T] slash (T^5 - 3)$ (soundness $256^(-33) = 2^(-264)$, nominal Grover exponent 132), about `9.2 MiB` on the wire. The same statement is also a committed zk-Hem relation (`zk_hem/blind_vc.rs`, §4), whose proof for 32 attributes is `239,507 B` (`233.89 KiB`) under the bounded-query Poseidon2 profile (tag 7 of @regimes), proved in `275–282 ms` and verified in `19.5–19.8 ms` on one core. No quantum Fiat–Shamir theorem is claimed for either presentation form; `ZkHemSoundnessAndPrivacy.lean` models the algebra of the zk-Hem masking layer.

*Private-policy multisignature (`zk_hem/multisig.rs`).* The same hiding prover supports a fixed-capacity zk-Multisig relation whose only public values are a policy address and a 32-byte message. Each of $N$ slots holds a member key with its policy path and a `JortsPoseidon2` signature whose full verification is constrained; a hidden `active` bit per slot, a prefix rule, strictly increasing member indices, and a range check on $sum "active" - t$ make the relation identical for every threshold, member count, and signing subset. Measured proofs: $N = 4$ in `1.12 s` (`25.7 ms` verify, `424,675 B`), $N = 8$ in `1.97 s` (`40.8 ms`, `440,131 B`), and $N = 16$ in `3.48 s` (`53.3 ms`, `700,491 B`).

