== Pillar 1: protocol, coding-theory, storage, and QROM spine

Foundational and protocol modules (`Goldilocks`, `Gf2Pow128`, `Gf128ClmulLoopAgreement`, `Keccak`, `Poseidon2`, `Fips205`, `HypertreeEufCma`, `BaggyAndRss256Security`, `JortsBeltLatchAndQuorumSwitching`, `InteractiveUcEngine`, `ThresholdMlDsaSecurity`, `Lattices`, `BlindVcQromSecurity`, `HemSlicedFoldBudget`) establish bijective sponge encodings, multi-turn latch safety, conditional ML-DSA view privacy, `Q192` Gaussian HVZK, cylinder-sliced [CWYZ26] certificates, and sequential UC composition [Can01]. Three cross-module custody boundaries are stated explicitly in Lean: `InteractiveUcEngine` (`IdealOutputBoundary`) assumes an ideal signing interface delivering exact oracle bytes, `RssProductionProfile` models enrolled-key partial (`Except`) signing, and `SeparatedCustody*` (`CompleteMediationBoundary`, `SeparatedCustodyCollision`) models single-host mediated token capability; unifying these three slices into one distributed multi-host UC refinement remains future work.

*Redesigned ideal functionalities (`Spec/`).* The specification layer was rewritten so that each ideal machine states what a deployment is entitled to, not what a proof happens to deliver. `IdealMigrate` has no free validity predicate: it mints only for an enrolled key, with a derived nullifier and a key-ownership condition, for the snapshot entitlement, and proves a single claim per key and conservation. `IdealCred` has port-tagged inputs and corruption as state; possession of a credential is a capability handle minted by issuance, epochs are checked, and the TOPRF table is gated by 4-of-5 approvals with a quota and counter window. `IdealCustody` binds a `deploymentId`, counts a quorum over recorded approvals of the exact intent, and admits adaptive corruption below threshold. `IdealAggregate` defines a valid break as a hash collision among recorded queries and states `AggregateExtractorSound` as a proposition indexed by an error and an extractor class (accept-all is refuted for straight-line extractors). `IdealSign` emits only candidates that verify under the enrolled key, on strictly increasing epochs, and burns the epoch of a failed signing; the production latch refines it unconditionally. `Assumptions` builds each game from a primitive, a sampler, and an adversary with separated coins and quantifies hardness over an explicit adversary class. Beneath them, `LawfulField` carries `inv_zero`, `PrimeOrderGroup` carries `n_prime`, parameters are checked line by line against the Rust constants, and the aggregation AIR rows, share-level MPC model, and round-1 commitment are rebased onto the canonical Poseidon2-12 profile. The specification, tactic, mathematics, coding, and oracle layers pass the import policy with no `import all` and no upward import; the layers above them still carry `import all` edges. These are sharper statements, not closed reductions: no real protocol yet refines its ideal machine unconditionally (migration holds under a circuit-soundness premise with only a toy instance, credentials have no protocol state machine in Lean, custody holds on runs satisfying three unproved assumptions, and no extractor-soundness instance exists for the deployed Hem verifier), and hardness for an unrestricted adversary class is refutable, so it is meaningful only for classes this development cannot yet delimit. What is unconditional is the event bound $Pr["forgery"] <= Pr["ITSR"] + Pr["TCR"] + Pr["PRE"]$ over extractor events.

*Staged leaf modules.* Further leaf modules, each compiled with warnings as errors against the library base with no `import all` and swept to the same `{propext, Quot.sound}` allowlist, await registration in the umbrella build; the whole-library gates above do not yet cover them.
- `WelchBerlekampDecoder`: a Reed–Solomon unique decoder for any lawful field, sound with no hypotheses, complete for distinct points and $k + 2e <= n$, equal to the Guruswami–Sudan specification list in that radius, and costing at most $2(n+2)^3 + 5(n+2)^2$ field operations in a counting monad.
- `BlockCompressedOracle` and `PrefixMerkleExtraction`: the Fourier step of Zhandry's compressed oracle [Zha19] for $w$-bit blocks, where a query mixes only labels differing in the queried block (instantiated at $w = 256$ without enumeration), and a two-phase Merkle extractor that reads only the commit-phase log, failing on at most $(d + Q_2 + Q_1)(3 Q_1 + 1) slash 2^w$ of oracles for a classical adversary. The link to the library's own oracle definitions is by law premises until their equation lemmas are exported.
- `MacaroonAttenuation`: appending a caveat only narrows the authorized set, any accepted edit of a caveat chain yields a prefix-free forgery of the chained MAC, and a discharge is bound to one presenting token.
- `PorsFpSecurity`: the subset-cover bound of §2.3.
- `SacrificeFreezeSample` and `RepetitionCutAndChoose`: a sacrifice whose transcript is frozen before the challenge accepts a wrong transcript on at most one of $abs(FF)^tau$ challenge vectors, $< 2^(-137)$ for ML-DSA's $q$ at $tau >= 6$, while sampling before freezing accepts always; and the three-challenge cut-and-choose behind `BlindVC` presentations is bounded by $2^(-263)$ at 33 repetitions, one bit above its leading term.
- `TapRowParityBias`: the signed count of a linear test is a product over trees with no balance premise; row 0 of the deployed tap matrix has bias strictly between $2^(-118)$ and $2^(-117)$, and 61 of its 64 trees violate the $<= 2047$ balance premise that the top-level bound still assumes for that row.
- `TwoSlotHwmCrashModel`: under a crash model in which one sector beneath an unsynced write is arbitrary, the paged layout of §2.4 never recovers below an acknowledged counter for any sector size dividing the page, whereas a layout with both slots in one sector loses state to a single tear. The MAC is an assumption (`NoForge`) and the concrete encodings are not yet instantiated.

== Pillar 2: Safe-Rust MIR refinement and live runtime welds

Charon and Aeneas translate `qsag-verified` and `qsag-whir-verified` into Lean under `0 FunsExternal`. Beyond `RealSpecFieldWeld.lean` and `RealSpecKeccakWeld.lean`, `SpongeFraming.lean` proves *unconditional short-nonempty `cSHAKE256` MIR refinement* (`sponge_new_cshake256_short_nonempty_absorbed`, `cshake256_32_short_nonempty_refines`, `cshake256_short_nonempty_xof_from_extracted_state`) for all customization strings of length $1 <= abs("cust") <= 31$, discharging the rate-block framing bridge across every 14–26-byte `"QSAG.*"` domain tag (multi-block messages $> 135$ B remain an inductive extension). Production crates invoke these extracted functions directly on live critical paths (`VerifiedWaistbandRootGuard`, `BeltLatchKernel`, `ConsumingOpeningGuard2Lane`, `VerifiedBeltQuorumGuard`, `FtsEpochGuard`, `MlDsaReleaseGate`, `HdCommittedZeroTest`, `StatefulVelocityGuard`, `HybridPinnedEnvelope`, and the six WHIR gates in `qsag-agg`). While all 9 source hashes in `qsag-whir-verified` match the current `extraction-provenance.json`, `MirSpecBridgeCore.lean` interfaces via hand-authored Lean records alongside Aeneas `QsagVerified.Funs`, and 4 hardened guard files in `qsag-verified` await fresh Charon/Aeneas re-extraction in the repository. That re-extraction has been reproduced end to end in a staging tree: the extractor binaries match the recorded SHA-256 values, a control extraction of the unmodified sources reproduces the committed output up to source line numbers, the provenance check passes for both crates (23 and 9 source files), and the MIR axiom audit accepts all `6,662` declarations. In it `Shake256Sponge::absorb` asserts that the sponge is not yet squeezing, as production does, and the old theorem that absorbing after a squeeze is a no-op is replaced by one that it fails (`shake256_absorb_after_squeezing_fails`). A generated coverage manifest classifies the crate's `235` public functions: 63 have a production call path, 84 are reached only by Lean proofs or spec welds (Goldilocks and tower arithmetic among them), 56 by tests, and 32 are unreferenced. The classification is lexical.

== Pillar 3: refreshed ELF64 byte pins, `McRefine`, and strict CT screening

`ConstantTimeIsa.lean`, `ElfBinaryVerify.lean`, and `McRefine.lean` model instruction semantics and three-way leakage taint (PC/branches, memory addresses, variable-latency operands, and dirty upper-YMM state) directly over compiled `.rlib` ELF64 `.text` slices. `CompiledRlibFixtures.lean`, `ElfFixtureVerify.lean`, and `FaultAndSpeculativeSafety.lean` match *`28/28` live Rust 1.99.1 exported `qsag_asm_*` assembly kernels* (`19/19` x86-64 = `1,935` instructions + `9/9` AArch64 = `1,420` instructions; `3,355` decoded instructions, `0` byte drift under kernel-bytes fixture binding, plus `5/5` standalone `SimdBinaryEquiv` fixtures and `5` `SydTv` bounded-loop certificates), while `McRefine.lean` proves *whole-kernel mathematical I/O refinement for `26/28`* exported assembly kernels (leaving the two `64×256` transpose kernels to noninterference/looping certificates). Across the entire compiled `libqsag_asm.rlib` crypto surface—including root-level dispatchers and Rust-mangled `avx512`, `gf256_simd`, `sha2_hw`, and `ntt_simd` routines whose Lean decoder coverage remains future work—our strict ELF instruction screen (`audit_elf_ct.py --strict-rust-branches`, backed by `124` `#tooth` auditor self-tests) verified *`0` constant-time violations across all `92` x86-64 and `65` AArch64 compiled crypto symbols*, and release `objdump` disassembly confirmed *`0` `xmm`/`ymm`/`zmm` stack spills* in both 8-way `AVX-512` Keccak and Seiler `AVX2`/`AVX-512` ML-DSA NTT kernels. The screen is run on four canonical archives of the current release—x86-64 and AArch64, each with and without `std`—built with one codegen unit, the workspace release setting; all four report `0` taint errors, alongside 19 native x86-64 kernels, 7 Linux and 7 Apple AArch64 kernels, Windows and Apple x86-64 cross-assemblies, and 15 negative self-tests that must fail. The codegen-unit count matters: the same sources built with 16 units emit 16 objects with different outlining and trip `489` screen errors, so the gate pins the profile it certifies. AArch64 code is cross-compiled and inspected, not executed on Arm hardware. `SpirvSimtGpuEquiv.lean` proves SPIR-V codec roundtrip, Wave32 confluence, and modeled SIMT noninterference.

== Pillar 4: bare-metal Zen 5 cosimulation, `wasm32` portability, and ground-truth gates

We executed bare-metal Linux x86-64 `ptrace` single-step silicon cosimulation (`isa_cosim_silicon.py`) on an *AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX PRO 370 (Zen 5)* (`XCR0 & 0xE6 == 0xE6`), passing *`59/59` x86-64 test cases across `7,184` random + boundary vectors with `0` skips and `0` register/memory mismatches*, alongside `8/8` mandatory-feature `AVX-512` differential suites verified on *both* native Zen 5 silicon and Intel SDE 10.13.1 (`-spr`, including exhaustive $65,536$-pair Wiedemann `GF(2^8)` parity and all NIST ACVP ML-DSA-44/65/87 KATs). All eight core custody/crypto crates (`qsag-asm`, `qsag-addr`, `qsag-policy`, `qsag-fts`, `qsag-hd`, `qsag-cred`, `qsag-quorum`, `qsag-core`) compile with `0` errors and `0` warnings on 32-bit `wasm32-unknown-unknown --no-default-features`. Complementing silicon execution, `50` differential and property tests in `validation/` cross-check QSAG against 17 independent upstream cryptography crates and `ExternalSpecOracle.lean` (including end-to-end `QS-A`/`QS-B`/`HemSpdzSeedQuorum` $->$ `Hem` `BciksJohnsonQrom128` verification), the strict `spec-oracle` suite passed `1,952` differential vectors (`5,416` total checks, `0` mismatches) and caught `13/13` production clause-drop mutations, and six-kernel replay checks all `130 + 71 + 57` challenge theorems. Native AArch64 `ptrace` execution remains separate from cross-compiled ELF verification.

