== Protocol latency, unconditional proof sizes, and engineering trade-offs

@protobench summarizes protocol benchmarks under unconditional `BciksJohnsonQrom128` (`Intel Core i9-12900 (Alder Lake)`, `AMD EPYC-Milan (Zen 3)`, and `AMD Radeon RX 6750 XT (RDNA 2)`). For $K = 8$ signers under wire-v6 affine-monomial folding, `JortsPoseidon2` aggregates serialize to `291,352 B` and `16`-layer stateless `CorduroyPoseidon2` aggregates serialize to `446,254 B`; a full 128-signer Jorts shard ($n_v = 24$) serializes to `445,566 B` (`435.1 KiB`), proves in `3.67–3.73 s` on 22 CPU workers or `1.56–1.57 s` with the Vulkan backend, and verifies in `2.7–3.0 ms` on all workers (`6.2–6.4 ms` on one core); CPU and GPU provers emit the same bytes. With path deduplication and batched extension arithmetic, multi-shard `BciksJohnsonQrom128` verification reaches *`5.75–7.90 ms`* for `4 × 32 = 128` subnet signers (`1,589,980 B`) and `6.9–7.3 ms` multi-core (`34–39 ms` 1-core) for `4 × 128 = 512` signers. On the custody signing path, pre-staged Mode 3B Jorts (`≈ 1.0 ms` local RAM, `≈ 81 ms` simulated WAN) and loopback QS-A (`≈ 0.56 ms`) collapse online signing to one opening round, whereas `ConfidentialWireChannel` over ext4 WAL `fsync` measured `4.37 ms` (QS-A 2-of-3) and `5.21 ms` (QS-B 3-of-7). Conversely, full-hypertree dishonest-majority symmetric MPC remains minutes-scale (`38,400` Boolean Beaver triples per unbatched Keccak-f[1600], and distributed ZKBoo/SPDZ full-suite runs hit 600 s CI ceilings under 12 GiB memory caps).

